A||||1||pHoOl||Stereo upgrades---- SeanH?||||1||
Z||000000||pHoOl||12-08-2000||10:00 PM||||I'm thinking of replacing my stock cassette player, due to lack of cooperation of the portable CD player and the converter... you get the idea.  <P>I would like to keep a stock look as much as possible.  Had the thought of getting a current CD player from a Grand Am or something, but have no idea on cost.  I'm assuming that if i do this, i am going to have to pay some big bucks.  <P>Are there any aftermarked systems i should be looking into?  I saw some of the Sony Xplodes that looked pretty cool, and i like the way that the glow red to match the rest of the dash.  <P>Recommendations?  <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/pHoOl_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Seanh||12-08-2000||11:35 PM||||Depends on about how much you are willing to spend. If you like the factory look, then I would go for a Grand Am Cd player or like. Generally, you could get one used for about $150.00. Most aftermarket ones start about there. If you are not going to use an amp, and like good high-quality sound, I would use an aftermarket. Maybe the Pioneer DIN & 1/2, they look really good in the Fiero. You might check around and see what you could get them for. Look at my post in the mall about them. You are probally not wanting to spend that much money, so you might want to check on Ebay. There are really some good deals on there. As far as aftermarket reccomendations. I would tend to say Sony, Pioneer. Some of the lower models are available at Wal-Mart, even the cheaper Pioneers sound great and are very good quality. Sony is just about the same, I am not a big fan of their looks, but its just personal preferance. Eclipse is a very good brand also, along with Kenwood's. If you ever want to listen to a musical radio station, stay away from Alpines (Great CD Player, horrid tuners). I can get the following brands of CD players in an order going out on the 16th of December: Boss, Legacy, Pioneer, Pyle, Pyramid, and Rampage.  I would reccomend a Pioneer. If you are willing to put more money into it, go with a DIN & 1/2 one, they look good in the Fiero. Check some local stereo shops if there are any, and see what they have for some Grand Am or like factory decks. They should be fairly reasonable.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/transsig.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>"It's always in the last place you look." Of course it is. Why would you keep looking after you've found it? Do people do this? Who and where are they?</I>||||reg||1
